1. Partial Index
Definition
A Partial Index is an index that only indexes documents that satisfy a specified filter condition.
In simple words:
"Don't index every document. Index only the documents I choose."


Example
Suppose your users collection contains:
{
    name: "Vasanth",
    email: "vasanth@gmail.com",
    status: "active"
}
{
    name: "Rahul",
    email: "rahul@gmail.com",
    status: "inactive"
}
{
    name: "Kumar",
    email: "kumar@gmail.com",
    status: "active"
}
Suppose you only want an index for active users.
You can create:
db.users.createIndex(
    { email: 1 },
    {
        partialFilterExpression: {
            status: "active"
        }
    }
)
Now the index contains only:
Vasanth → vasanth@gmail.com
Kumar   → kumar@gmail.com
Rahul is not included because:
status = "inactive"

Wildcard Index
Now let's look at Wildcard Index.
Definition
A Wildcard Index is an index that allows MongoDB to index unknown or arbitrary fields within documents, making it useful when documents have highly variable or dynamic structures.
In simple words:
"I don't know exactly which fields users will search, so index fields dynamically."

Example
Suppose your documents have different fields.
Document 1:
{
    name: "Laptop",
    brand: "Dell",
    price: 60000
}
Document 2:
{
    name: "Phone",
    manufacturer: "Samsung",
    storage: "256GB"
}
Document 3:
{
    name: "Camera",
    megapixels: 48,
    lens: "Wide"
}
The fields are different.
Creating individual indexes for every possible field could become difficult:
db.products.createIndex({ brand: 1 })
db.products.createIndex({ manufacturer: 1 })
db.products.createIndex({ storage: 1 })
db.products.createIndex({ megapixels: 1 })
db.products.createIndex({ lens: 1 })
Instead, you can use a Wildcard Index:
db.products.createIndex({
    "$**": 1
})
$** means:
Index fields throughout the document.










